uld not upon our country's  freedom in the Revolutionary War didn't  make everybody free but you know besides  that I want to ask you what do you think  difference would have been if Martin  Luther King jr. had not tragically been  assassinated well thank you for calling  if one day the King jr. had not been  assassinated and President Kennedy and  Liv and Robert Kennedy and many others I  think we would be much further down that  road toward the building of a truly  interracial democracy dr. Cain had  emerged as a moral leader  perhaps more than any other American of  our time he had the power to bring more  people together to do good not just  african-americans but all people  dr. King was the undisputed undisputed  symbol of what was good and right about  America and he wanted to move America  much father and with the helpful  individuals like President Kennedy  another we would have been able to do  some Charleston South Carolina your next  good morning for Congressman John Lewis  of Georgia good morning representative  Lewis and to the host I just want to say  thank you and I commend you for all your  hard work and also for all our  forefathers fathers dr. Martin Luther  King and all the participants of the  March and for everything that that  you've done for us I just want to say  thank you and I just wish that I was  there to see all the great things that  had happened for the March because it  just looked like it was so peaceful and  so calm thanks for the comment last call  from Houston Texas good morning good  morning good morning mr. Lewis this is a  mr. Stroh CA calling I'd like to thank  you very much for what you've done what  you've stood for 40 years I'm proud to  be an African American and I just like  to say to you sir I don't think there is  a civil rights movement today it just  seems as though African American people  have just given up and there's no fight  in them it seemed as though all of our  so-called civil rights organizations or  speechless today they're not dealing  with the issues pertaining to the  injustice is done to African Americans  particularly African American men thanks  for the call we'll get a response thank  you very much for calling I said earlier  and I said to you you must never give up  a giving a give out you have to continue  to hang in there another generation of  young people and  one called us up and told us to go in  start sitting in get on a bus and going  to free to ride a start marching we were  deeply inspired by the words to monitor  King jr. deeply inspired by our history  books of what we knew about the  Constitution the Declaration of  Independence the Bill of Rights and we  had to act we had to move and I think  today in America not just  african-american but all of us all of us  are just a little too quiet we look too  complacent we have to get involved  we need to register and we need to go  out and vote you need to get involved in  political scene and civil organization  doing things because it's the right  thing to do you know you don't need to  wait on someone to come down from  Atlanta from New York to to help from  another part of the country you have to  do it in the south during the early 60s  indigenous leaders emerge young people  high school students college students  just got out there and got in the way  they start pushing and pulling and  that's what people must do today do  marches work today  I think marches still work dr. Caine  used to say that's nothing more powerful  than the margin feet of a determined  people Congressman John Lewis who's been  in the US House of Representatives since  1986 and will be at the Lincoln Memorial  later today to commemorate the 40th  anniversary you're always gracious with  your time here at c-span thanks for  being with us - thank you very much  really been a pleasure to be with you  and thank you for your good and great  work
 

